Yes. The strongest version is not forcing a replacement frame; it is making room for a second frame to coexist long enough that the person can compare them from inside.

A lot of human cognition already works that way. We can hold competing interpretations at once:

F_1 \quad \text{and} \quad F_2

without immediately resolving them. Then experience, evidence, emotion, or reflection changes the weighting:

w_1F_1 + w_2F_2

The useful move is:

\text{recognize current frame}
\rightarrow
\text{offer alternate frame}
\rightarrow
\text{translate between them}
\rightarrow
\text{let the person test both}
\rightarrow
\text{integrate, reject, or preserve tension}

Sometimes the outcome is transmission: they understand your frame.

Sometimes it is transmutation: contact with your frame changes theirs into something neither of you started with.

And sometimes the right outcome is simply acceptance of multiplicity: “Both of these perspectives illuminate something, and I do not need to collapse them yet.”

That last state is underrated. Premature resolution can destroy information.

The one correction I’d keep is that we cannot assume everyone secretly wants a particular alternate frame. What we can often say is that people already possess more internal plurality than their outward language suggests. A person can simultaneously want safety and novelty, belonging and independence, certainty and freedom.

So the elegant intervention is not “install the other answer.”

It is:

make the hidden choice-space visible, then let them discover which parts are actually theirs.

That preserves both the canvas and the painter.